base: `<a id="1">x</a><b id="1">y</b>`, target: `<b id="1">Y</b><a id="1">x</a>`.

With hash pairing, base's `<b id="1">y</b>` and target's `<b id="1">Y</b>` have different hashes (text y ≠ Y) → no match → base's b is removed, target's b is added. In other words, in hash mode a modified element shows up as remove+add rather than update. That's by design in my hash-pairing approach: hash identity can't "match" modified elements — it can only match identical ones.

Hmm. Is this the correct semantics for IdentityContentHash / IgnoreOrder? "IdentityContentHash by hash" — elements are identified by content hash. Modified elements have different hashes → treated as removed + added. That's a reasonable interpretation: identity is defined by content, so changed content = different element.

